act_* &lt;= cfg_*
when frame_latch" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#ffe6cc;strokeColor=#d79b00;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="660" y="620" width="160" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="err" value="Error aggregation
CFG STARVE GEOM
SRC_STALL OUT_STALL" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="870" y="620" width="160" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="e8" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="axil" target="latch"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="e9" value="act_w/h/x/y/alpha/en" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="latch" target="win"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="e10" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="err" target="axil"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="n1" value="Three handshake domains, deliberately decoupled:

A  source -&gt; layer FIFO      tready = enable ? !full : 1
B  FIFO -&gt; cascade           pop, gated by pipe_en
C  cascade -&gt; sink           pipe_en = tready || !tvalid

Backpressure from A NEVER reaches C. A layer with no pixel
when its window opens is DROPPED, not waited for." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="1060" y="120" width="330" height="160" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n2" value="Latency, SOF in to SOF out:  2 + N cycles
  stage F, stage A, then one stage per layer." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="1060" y="300" width="330" height="55" as="geometry"/></mxCell></root></mxGraphModel></diagram><diagram id="2_Backpressure" name="2 Backpressure"><mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1000" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0"/><mxCell id="1" parent="0"/><mxCell id="t" value="Backpressure semantics" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="700" height="30" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="st" value="The one rule: input backpressure must never become output backpressure." style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="48" width="900" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="srcA" value="upstream
source" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="140" width="110" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="layA" value="axis_mixer_layer" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="240" y="140" width="150" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="fifoA" value="FIFO" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="240" y="240" width="150" height="50" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ea1" value="tvalid / tdata" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="srcA" target="layA"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="ea2" value="tready" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;exitX=0;exitY=0.8;entryX=1;entryY=0.8;" edge="1" parent="1" source="layA" target="srcA"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="ea3" value="push" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="layA" target="fifoA"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="dA" value="DOMAIN A - source into the layer

  assign tready = enable ? !fifo_full : 1'b1;

tready depends on FIFO space and NOTHING else.
Not on the raster, not on the sink.

The enable term is load bearing: a DISABLED layer
asserts tready permanently and discards. Backpressuring
a disabled layer wedges its source forever, and the
source has no way to know why." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="440" y="130" width="360" height="170" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="fifoB" value="FIFO head
px_valid, px_data" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="400" width="140" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="popB" value="pop = in_win &amp; lay_active
&amp; !dropped &amp; px_valid
&amp; pipe_en &amp; s0_valid" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="240" y="395" width="200" height="70" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="starveB" value="starve = in_win &amp; lay_active
&amp; !dropped &amp; !px_valid
&amp; pipe_en &amp; s0_valid" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="240" y="495" width="200" height="70" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="eb1" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="fifoB" target="popB"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="eb2" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="fifoB" target="starveB"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="dB" value="DOMAIN B - FIFO into the cascade

A pop happens only when the raster is inside the window
AND the layer is active AND a pixel is there.

If the raster wants a pixel and none is there, that is
STARVE, and starve does not stall -- it drops the layer
for the rest of the frame, flushes its FIFO and re-arms
at the next input SOF.

Why flush rather than skip one pixel: the FIFO contents
are now positionally wrong by however many pixels were
missed, and nothing in the stream says by how many.
There is no mid-frame resync. SOF is the only landmark." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="500" y="390" width="400" height="200" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="cascC" value="blend cascade
last stage v_q[N]" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="700" width="150" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="sinkC" value="downstream
sink" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="320" y="700" width="110" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ec1" value="tvalid = v_q[N]  (registered)" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="cascC" target="sinkC"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="ec2" value="tready" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;exitX=0;exitY=0.8;entryX=1;entryY=0.8;" edge="1" parent="1" source="sinkC" target="cascC"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="dC" value="DOMAIN C - the output, and the gate everything hangs off

  assign pipe_en = m_axis_tready || !m_axis_tvalid;

Read it as: the last stage may be overwritten if it is being
consumed, or if it holds nothing worth keeping.

Because every stage shifts under the SAME pipe_en, that one
condition propagates backwards for free -- the pipeline is a
single shift register. Either everything moves or nothing does.

The || !tvalid term is not an optimisation. Without it a sink
that holds tready low while the pipe is empty freezes the raster
for no reason, and you lose the fill time you needed to have data
ready the moment tready returns." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="500" y="660" width="430" height="210" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="must" value="pipe_en MUST gate every piece of datapath state:

  out_x / out_y      raster counters
  lay_pop[i]         FIFO pops
  stage F, stage A   capture and alpha
  acc_q, v_q, sof_q, eol_q, eof_q   every cascade stage

Miss the raster counter and the picture shears: the counter
runs ahead of the pixels. Miss a FIFO pop and you drop a
pixel and shift the layer by one for the rest of the frame." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=10;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="990" y="660" width="400" height="160" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="loop" value="NO COMBINATIONAL PATH FROM TREADY TO TVALID

tvalid is v_q[N], a flip-flop. pipe_en depends on tready, but
pipe_en only drives register ENABLES -- never the value of
tvalid. That is what keeps this legal AXI4-Stream and keeps
two of these back to back from forming a combinational loop." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="990" y="400" width="400" height="120" as="geometry"/></mxCell></root></mxGraphModel></diagram><diagram id="3_Pipeline" name="3 Pipeline"><mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1000" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0"/><mxCell id="1" parent="0"/><mxCell id="t" value="Pipeline and the pipe_en rail" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="700" height="30" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="st" value="One enable for the whole pipe. Latency 2 + N." style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="48" width="900" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="s0" value="raster
out_x, out_y
s0_valid/sof/eol/eof" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#ffe6cc;strokeColor=#d79b00;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="80" y="160" width="160" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="s1" value="F
capture FIFO read
pxf_q, alphaf_q, bgf_q" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="280" y="160" width="160" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ep1" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="s0" target="s1"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="s2" value="A
effective_alpha
la_q[0][i]" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="480" y="160" width="160" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ep2" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="s1" target="s2"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="s3" value="B0
acc_q[1] =
over(L0, bg)" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="680" y="160" width="160" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ep3" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="s2" target="s3"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="s4" value="B1
acc_q[2] =
over(L1, acc_q[1])" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="880" y="160" width="160" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ep4" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="s3" target="s4"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="s5" value="BN
acc_q[N] =
over(L(N-1), ...)" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="1080" y="160" width="160" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ep5" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="s4" target="s5"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="outp" value="m_axis
tvalid = v_q[N]" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="1280" y="175" width="130" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ep6" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="s5" target="outp"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="rail" value="pipe_en = m_axis_tready || !m_axis_tvalid" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#ffe6cc;strokeColor=#d79b00;fillColor=#ffcccc;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="80" y="330" width="1160" height="40" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="er0" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;endArrow=open;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;html=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="rail" target="s0"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="er1" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;endArrow=open;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;html=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="rail" target="s1"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="er2" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;endArrow=open;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;html=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="rail" target="s2"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="er3" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;endArrow=open;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;html=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="rail" target="s3"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="er4" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;endArrow=open;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;html=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="rail" target="s4"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="er5" value="" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;dashed=1;endArrow=open;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;html=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="rail" target="s5"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="n3" value="Every box above is enabled by the same rail. That is the whole
flow-control design, and its cost is that a stalled sink stalls
the entire pipe rather than letting earlier stages keep filling.

The alternative is an elastic pipeline: valid/ready and a skid
buffer per stage. More area, more state, and worth it only when
there are several independent stall sources. Here there is exactly
one -- the sink -- so the monolithic rail is the right trade." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="80" y="410" width="520" height="130" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n4" value="WHY STAGE F EXISTS AND DOES NOTHING

The layer pixel comes out of a block RAM. BRAM clock-to-out is
most of a cycle at 148.5 MHz. Feeding it straight into the alpha
multiply left 14 levels of logic in what remained and missed
setup by 0.27 ns.

Stage F captures the read and does no arithmetic, so stage A
starts from a flip-flop. If you are hitting timing on the read
path, this is the cheap fix -- one register stage, one cycle." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="640" y="410" width="470" height="150" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n5" value="WHY A CASCADE AND NOT A TREE

Porter-Duff 'over' IS associative, so a log2(N) tree looks
tempting. It does not work with an RGB-only accumulator:
combining two layers before compositing them onto the
background needs the pair's COMPOSITE alpha, which means
carrying premultiplied RGBA through the tree.

The cascade avoids that entirely, because the bottom of the
stack is the opaque background. The accumulator is therefore
opaque by construction at every stage, and 'over' onto an
opaque destination needs only the top layer's alpha.

Cost: depth N instead of log2(N). At N=4..16 and one multiply
per stage that is latency, not frequency." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="1140" y="410" width="400" height="200" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n6" value="A layer that is not contributing arrives at its stage with
alpha = 0, and over() with alpha 0 returns the accumulator
bit-exactly. Absence needs no special case anywhere." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="80" y="570" width="520" height="60" as="geometry"/></mxCell></root></mxGraphModel></diagram><diagram id="4_Layer_FSM" name="4 Layer FSM"><mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1000" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0"/><mxCell id="1" parent="0"/><mxCell id="t" value="Layer input FSM - re-establishing alignment from evidence" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="800" height="30" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="st" value="Nothing links a layer's stream to the output raster except a count. SOF is the only landmark." style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="48" width="1000" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="wait" value="WAIT_SOF
armed = 0
discard input" style="ellipse;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=12;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="200" y="200" width="170" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="strm" value="STREAM
push to FIFO
count px, ln" style="ellipse;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=12;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="620" y="200" width="170" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="f1" value="beat &amp;&amp; TUSER
(SOF beat is pixel 0, kept)" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="wait" target="strm"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="f2" value="clean end of frame
(last px of last line)
NO flush" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=1;curved=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="strm" target="wait"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="f3" value="geom_bad -&gt; flush + geom_err" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;html=1;jettySize=auto;orthogonalLoop=1;fontSize=10;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;curved=1;strokeColor=#b85450;" edge="1" parent="1" source="strm" target="wait"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"></mxGeometry></mxCell><mxCell id="gb" value="geom_bad = accept &amp;&amp; (
     TLAST != (px_cnt == W-1)
  || TUSER != (state != STREAM) )

Either means the source and the SIZE register disagree
about the shape of a frame. Every pixel after that point
lands in the wrong place and stays wrong, so the layer is
flushed and re-armed rather than allowed to continue." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=10;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="880" y="170" width="420" height="140" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n7" value="WHY A CLEAN END OF FRAME DOES NOT FLUSH

The FIFO still holds pixels the output has not consumed. The
next frame queues up behind them, and that prefill is exactly
what keeps a layer positioned at x = 0 from starving on the
first line of the next frame.

armed therefore stays high across the boundary. Only a flush
-- soft reset, starve, geometry change, or disable -- disarms." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="200" y="380" width="480" height="140" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n8" value="ARMED IS NOT ACTIVE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A REAL BUG

armed  : this layer has seen a SOF and is delivering pixels.
active : this layer is taking part in the current output frame.

active is set ONLY at an output frame boundary:

    if (frame_latch)
        lay_active[i] &lt;= lay_armed[i] &amp;&amp; want_en[i] &amp;&amp; !lay_flush[i];

The mixer consumes a layer POSITIONALLY -- one pop per output
pixel inside the window -- so wherever the first pop happens is
where the layer's top-left corner lands.

A layer that armed mid-frame would have its pixel 0 popped part
way along a line. And because it then supplies exactly as many
pixels per frame as the window consumes, that offset NEVER works
itself out. The layer sits skewed by a fixed amount forever.

Holding off until the boundary guarantees the FIFO head is pixel
zero when the window first opens, because nothing pops an
inactive layer." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=10;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="740" y="380" width="560" height="290" as="geometry"/></mxCell></root></mxGraphModel></diagram><diagram id="5_FIFO_sizing" name="5 FIFO sizing"><mxGraphModel dx="1400" dy="900" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1000" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0"/><mxCell id="1" parent="0"/><mxCell id="t" value="FIFO depth, and why one full layer line" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="700" height="30" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="st" value="The output consumes a layer w pixels out of every W, on h lines out of H. The FIFO absorbs the difference." style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="40" y="48" width="1100" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="canvas" value="" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#666666;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="80" y="120" width="500" height="290" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="clab" value="canvas W x H" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="90" y="125" width="150" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="winA" value="layer A
x = 0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="80" y="190" width="190" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="winB" value="layer B
x &gt; 0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="350" y="260" width="170" height="90" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n9" value="WORST CASE IS A WINDOW AT x = 0

Layer B has the whole span from the start of the output line
until x reaches its left edge to push pixels. Layer A has none:
the instant the line begins, the mixer starts popping, so every
pixel that line needs must ALREADY be buffered.

Hence depth &gt;= layer width. One full line is the safe sizing,
and 2048 covers any width up to 1920.

Over a whole frame the rates balance exactly -- the layer
supplies w*h and the mixer consumes w*h -- so the FIFO is
absorbing INTRA-frame burstiness, not a rate mismatch. If the
rates genuinely differ you get starve, and no depth fixes it." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="640" y="120" width="470" height="210" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="occ" value="FIFO occupancy over one output line, layer at x = 0" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="80" y="450" width="500" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ax" value="" style="shape=line;direction=north;strokeColor=#666666;html=1;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="110" y="480" width="10" height="180" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="ax2" value="" style="shape=line;strokeColor=#666666;html=1;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="110" y="655" width="900" height="10" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="plot" value="drains at 1 px/cycle while the raster is inside the window
refills during the rest of the line and during blanking" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;align=center;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="200" y="500" width="600" height="40" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="wave" value="" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=none;strokeColor=#82b366;strokeWidth=2;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="130" y="545" width="320" height="100" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="wave2" value="" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;fillColor=none;strokeColor=#82b366;strokeWidth=2;dashed=1;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="450" y="545" width="480" height="100" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="wl1" value="pop w pixels
back to back" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;align=center;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="180" y="580" width="220" height="40" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="wl2" value="no pops -- source refills" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;align=center;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="560" y="580" width="260" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="wl3" value="depth" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="550" width="50" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="wl4" value="time within one output line" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="450" y="665" width="250" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n10" value="FIRST-WORD FALL-THROUGH IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE

The consumer decides whether to pop based on whether data is
there (px_valid). It cannot afford a read-latency cycle inside
the blend pipeline, and it cannot speculatively pop.

At 2048 x 32 x N the storage has to be block RAM, which has
registered output. So: BRAM plus a prefetch register.

  mem_rd_en = (rd_ptr != wr_ptr) &amp;&amp; (!rd_valid || rd_en);

That single condition covers all four combinations of rd_valid
and rd_en with no state machine: fetch whenever memory holds
something and the prefetch slot is empty or being emptied." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="1140" y="120" width="430" height="200" as="geometry"/></mxCell><mxCell id="n11" value="Pointers carry one bit more than the address so full and empty
are distinguishable without sacrificing an entry:

  mem_count = wr_ptr - rd_ptr;
  full      = (mem_count == P_DEPTH);

level reports mem_count + rd_valid, so a FIFO holding exactly
one prefetched pixel does not report empty." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=10;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="1140" y="350" width="430" height="130" as="geometry"/></mxCell></root></mxGraphModel></diagram></mxfile>